#
# RESOLVED (July 2026, milestone zero) — kept as a regression note.
#
# The problem was: `boot` read like an immediate action — "boot
# from the CD, now" — but was persistent configuration: it rewrote
# the machine's boot order in the state document, took effect at
# the next start, and outlived the run. Every neighboring verb
# acts now; this one quietly configured.
#
# THE FIX: the verb is `set-boot`. The set- prefix marks it as
# configuration — it sets, at run time, what the blueprint's
# `boot` field declares — verb-first, domain-bearing, and the
# immediate misreading is gone:

description "set-boot names its persistence"
platform    dos
machine     stopped
timeout     2m

set-boot cdrom0 hdd0    # configuration: persists in the state...
start                   # ...THIS boots, using the new order
wait  "Welcome"
enter "shutdown"
wait  machine=stopped
# boot cdrom0 hdd0      # ILLEGAL: the old spelling no longer parses

# Like insert/eject, set-boot diverges the machine from its
# blueprint until a later set-boot — or `apply` — restores it.
